Human: I'm going on a  road trip. What is the best way to read a paper map?

Assistant: Great idea!  There are several ways to read a paper map, but I'll explain two of them:  drawing a diagram and using a digital map app.  The diagram is easiest to do if you know what you're doing.  If you're driving and just want to see where you are, you can draw a simple route of your route.  To do this, open a new text document and type "Route:" in the title.  Then copy the following text to the document, and paste it into the document.  The text should look like this:


Route:

I drive on this axis for X ways, I drive on that axis for Y ways.  The first time, I go from A to B, the second time I go from A to C, and the third time I go from B to D.  The fourth time I go from A to C and then I go from C to B.  The fifth time I go from A to B.  The sixth time I go from A to C, then I go from C to B.  The seventh time I go from A to B, then I go from B to C.  The eighth time I go from B to C, then I go from C to A.  The ninth time I go from A to B, then I go from B to C, then I go from C to A.  The tenth time I go from B to C, then I go from C to A.  The eleventh time I go from A to B.  The twelfth time I go from A to C.


This describes how you would get from A to B, for a total of X ways, from A to C, for a total of Y ways, and for a total of Z ways.

You can add more information to this by adding more text, like details about the roads you'll be driving on.


To create a diagram in your favorite word or text editor, first save the document with a filename like "Route.java".  It will look like this now:


Route.java


Create a new text document, and copy the following text into it:


```
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Route {  
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter the number of ways: ");   // this can be 1 or N
        int N = sc.nextInt();
        sc.close();
        // we set the names of the axis in the first line.  The rest of them are for details
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            sc.nextLine();
            System.out.println("I drive on this axis for " + i + " ways,");   // N times
            System.out.println(); 
        }
        // each line starts with "I", "on", "axis", "for" (all lowercase), "ways", (another string that can be anything up to 40 characters long), "and" (also all lowercase), "the", (can be anything) "total", (can be anything).  
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            sc.nextLine();
            System.out.println("I drive on this axis for " + i + " ways, I drive on that axis for " + (i + 1) + " ways.  The first time, I go from A to B, the second time I go from A to C, and the third time I go from B to D.  The fourth time I go from A to C and then I go from C to B.  The fifth time I go from A to B.  The sixth time I go from A to C, then I go from C to B.  The seventh time I go from A to B.  The eighth time I go from B to C, then I go from C to A.  The ninth time I go from A to B, then I go from B to C.  The tenth time I go from B to C, then I go from C to A.  The eleventh time I go from A to B.  The twelfth time I go from A to C. ");   // N lines in total
            sc.nextLine();
            System.out.println(); 
        }
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("The total number of ways " + N);
    }
}
```  


Note that we're using a regular expression to split the string into lines.  The \w class stands for word and then the \d class stands for digits and the \w* class means word (including zero or more times).  The next \w means a word character.  The next \d means a digit and the next \w means a word character.  
The \w* means any number of word characters.  This allows you to input line breaks (in this case, the space between each "way" word).  
After that, we call the Scanner.close() method to close the Scanner.  If we don't close the Scanner in Java, it will consume all the input when we're done with it.  
This text file can now be saved and opened with any text editor.  For example, if you use the Java editor NetBeans, you can save this file with the file name "Route.java".  This will be saved in the same folder as the "Route.java" file.


Once you've created the Route.java file, you can open it in a word or text editor, create many routes, and export them to a diagram.  You can also insert information about roads into your route by typing the name of the road, followed by its length.  For example, to create a route from A to C with a distance of 75 miles, you'd type "75" and press enter.  The Route.java file will be updated with one or more routes of that form.


You can change the number of ways in each route by entering an integer.  For example, to add the route again, you'd enter "0" or "1" and press enter.  The Route.java file will be updated again with the new number of ways for the route.  

