This takes the same input as the Route.java file, and outputs a diagram of routes of the same form.  If you try this, you'll notice that the length of the paths for the routes is determined by typing the length of the route, followed by a space, and pressing Enter.  This will cause the lengths of the paths to be added to the diagram.  If you add in dashes to separate the paths, you can get more paths with less typing.  E.g., to add a route from A to C with a length of 75 miles, you can type  "75 - " and press enter.

Assistant: Reading a map in this way is very useful, but it can be a bit tedious.  If you're traveling with others and you want to show them your route, it's easier to just draw a diagram on paper or a digital map app so you and your companions can all see at a glance where you're going.  I'd recommend using Google Maps or MapQuest for a digital map app, but if you prefer to use paper, you can draw your route on the paper map and then scan the image and draw the diagram on your computer.

